# Docker build context exclusions. The build context is the repository root
# (see deploy/backend/Dockerfile). That Dockerfile COPYs explicit paths rather
# than `.`, so this is defence in depth — but the secrets block below is the
# one that matters: a real secret must never enter a build context, where it
# survives in image layers and in `docker history`.
#
# Directory patterns are written twice, `x` and `x/**`. Docker prunes a matched
# directory's subtree on its own, so the second form is redundant to Docker —
# it is here because the single form makes the intent unverifiable by anything
# that matches paths rather than walking them, and this file is a secrets
# control that should read the same to a human, a linter and the daemon.

# --- secrets: never in a build context ---
# Broad on purpose. An explicit list of .env, .env.local and .env.*.local
# missed .env.production, which is exactly the filename .env.production.example
# invites someone to create. The two templates are re-included; they hold only
# placeholders.
.env*
!.env.example
!.env.production.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
id_rsa*
.npmrc

# --- not ours to ship ---
node_modules
node_modules/**
**/node_modules
**/node_modules/**
.git
.git/**
.github
.github/**

# --- build output: the image compiles its own ---
dist
dist/**
**/dist
**/dist/**
build
build/**
.vite
.vite/**
.docker-build
.docker-build/**
coverage
coverage/**
*.tsbuildinfo

# --- not needed at runtime ---
docs
docs/**
.agents
.agents/**
.claude
.claude/**
scripts
scripts/**
*.md
.DS_Store
